{"primaryContentSections":[{"kind":"declarations","declarations":[{"platforms":["Linux"],"tokens":[{"text":"@frozen","kind":"attribute"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"struct","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"Quadtree","kind":"identifier"},{"text":"<","kind":"text"},{"text":"Element","kind":"genericParameter"},{"text":">","kind":"text"}],"languages":["swift"]}]},{"content":[{"text":"Overview","anchor":"overview","type":"heading","level":2},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"The rectangle is split into four equal quadrants, and each quadrant again, until every leaf"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"holds at most "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"bucketCapacity"},{"type":"text","text":" elements (Finkel and Bentley, “Quad Trees: A Data Structure for"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"Retrieval on Composite Keys”, 1974). A search visits only the cells that meet its region."},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"Cells and elements live in flat arrays; a removal that leaves a cell’s quadrants holding no"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"more than one bucket merges them back. Cells at depth 24 never split, so any number of"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"elements may share one point."}],"type":"paragraph"},{"code":["var map = Quadtree<String>(bounds: BoundingBox(lower: [0, 0], upper: [100, 100]))","map.insert(\"café\", at: [10, 20])","map.insert(\"library\", at: [12, 22])","map.insert(\"station\", at: [80, 75])","map.nearest(to: [11, 19])?.element                          \/\/ \"café\"","map.items(within: 5, of: [11, 21]).count                    \/\/ 2","map.items(in: BoundingBox(lower: [50, 50], upper: [100, 100])).map(\\.element)   \/\/ [\"station\"]"],"type":"codeListing","syntax":"swift"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"A point on the line between two quadrants belongs to the upper one. The elements and their"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type"